<section image-layout="right" id="19s0d1lldym4jj5"><img class="accent-image" id="46ohezSZ9U" type="image" alt="futuristic office with glowing screens and people working alongside AI interfaces, soft blue light atmosphere" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/9yRWG0C4HaE3nCIp5QUzD.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>AI Effects on the Labor Market: Is AI Disrupting the Future of Work?</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="oqdr1yljq3pgaq4"><div class="body"><h1>How to Read This Presentation</h1><p>This Deep Dive explains how AI is reshaping the labor market from first principles. Sections are designed to be read in order and assume no prior background knowledge.</p><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item id="iOJpN"><h4>Our Goal</h4><p> Give you a clear map of the mechanisms at play — automation, augmentation, and new-task creation </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="Z6uRz"><h4>Scope</h4><p> Omissions are intentional — we focus on the past, present, and next 6–24 months, not AGI scenarios</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="m70en8oe7tpsv8e"><div class="body"><h1>Table of Contents</h1><smart-layout variant="processSteps" numbered="true" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="aDKlb"><h4>The Mechanics That Drive AI's Impact on Labor</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="zdDEV"><h4>Exposure</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="esPBn"><h4>Adoption</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="2w6Pm"><h4>Labor Market Response 1: Augmentation</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="DqGrH"><h4>Labor Market Response 2: Displacement</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="pb9je"><h4>Labor Market Response 3: Reinstatement</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="3zpN_"><h4>The AI-Work Feedback Cycle</h4></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="right" id="hvshfe69x7x8faz"><img class="accent-image" id="IoA6ykbECL" type="image" alt="large bold number 01 on a dark navy background with abstract circuit lines, minimal modern design" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/klUct2sdpKLnaR1dqOpG8.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>01 The Mechanics That Drive AI's Impact on Labor</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="f87kjbtlf6sg0t9"><div class="body"><img id="JBgsC" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/QdGUaRFSVYeVktpN6Z2M_.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>Experts Agree AI Will Transform Jobs, but Disagree on How</h1><smart-layout variant="quoteBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="SeZLA"><h4>Daron Acemoglu (Nobel Laureate)</h4><p> "We're still going to have journalists, financial analysts, HR employees." </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="fjVcS"><h4>Jensen Huang (Nvidia CEO)</h4><p> "You won't lose your job to AI, but you will lose your job to someone who uses AI." </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="17Unv"><h4>Dario Amodei (Anthropic CEO)</h4><p> "AI could wipe out half of all entry-level white-collar jobs."</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="asa4h9pbt8bamzh"><div class="body"><h1>What Is a Job?</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="C_LiG" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/glUNkHbEmv5PBzFt0kzxb.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>At its core, a job is a bundle of tasks. AI almost never wipes out whole jobs overnight — it affects <i>tasks</i> first.</p><ul><li>A <b>doctor</b> diagnoses, orders tests, interprets results, and coordinates with specialists</li><li>A <b>lawyer</b> drafts contracts, researches case law, argues motions, and advises clients</li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="fzeul76ki771anu"><div class="body"><img id="Am2jJ" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/LLcGThYn93b4dqi8xaiWz.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>A Three-Layer Framework for AI's Impact on Jobs</h1><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="SzShB"><h4>Layer 1 — Exposure</h4><p> Which tasks in a job are susceptible to automation, augmentable, or resistant? </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="0-4eO"><h4>Layer 2 — Adoption</h4><p> How quickly and in what form will organizations deploy AI? </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="xD6BW"><h4>Layer 3 — Labor Market Response</h4><p> What is the net effect on employment, wages, and new job creation?</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="wdyulxu6mg64ap4"><div class="body"><h1>The Scale of Potential Impact</h1><img id="tVd8Y" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/MFY7Zy5XzEBm1wquh-qN-.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="-lRNH" label="60%"><h4>U.S. Labor Share</h4><p>Of the $31T U.S. economy, nearly 60% is labor costs</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="vSk98" label="300M"><h4>Jobs Transformed</h4><p>~27% of OECD positions globally</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="qUKog" label="71%"><h4>Public Concern</h4><p>U.S. adults worried AI will "put too many people out of work permanently" (Aug 2025)</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="left" id="n17985et2nawo1i"><img class="accent-image" id="UfNcs2G351" type="image" alt="bold number 02 glowing on a dark background with abstract data wave patterns, blue tones" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/wDg_5BixI-tm4rJgSJmp1.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>02 Layer 1: Exposure</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="yydbueudp0adwpp"><div class="body"><h1>The Exposure Index</h1><columns col-widths="[50,50]"><div><h4>What It Measures</h4><p>An "exposure index" estimates (0–100) to what extent the tasks in your job can be assisted or performed by current or near-term AI. The higher the score, the more your job might be impacted.</p></div><div><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxes" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="f59vd"><h4>Task-to-Ability Mapping (AIOE)</h4><p> Identifies tasks in a job, estimates which AI can handle, and weights by importance </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="-UbPN"><h4>Anthropic's Economic Index</h4><p> Analyzes 1M+ Claude conversations — currently 44% of AI usage is for computer and mathematical tasks</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="okl76yewgyyh6lk"><div class="body"><h1>Which Jobs Are Most (and Least) Exposed?</h1><smart-layout variant="solidJoinedBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item id="MsCOw"><h4>🔴 Most Exposed</h4><p> Genetic counselors, financial examiners, actuaries, accountants, judges, procurement clerks — text-heavy, information-processing, and communication tasks score highest </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="h27Dm"><h4>🟢 Least Exposed</h4><p> Dancers, fitness trainers, roofers, electricians, landscapers, athletes — embodied, on-site, and dexterous tasks score lowest</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="58ypjl7r0gkaam8"><div class="body"><h1>AI Performance vs. Human Baseline (2012–2024)</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="NtdQx" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/V8m2vgt8lQ-Eymmn9KSJU.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>AI has surpassed the human baseline in most cognitive benchmark categories by 2024.</p><ul><li>Image classification, multitask language understanding, PhD science questions — all now <b>above human baseline</b></li><li>Clerical and back-office roles (document processing, transcription, standardized comms) are among the most automatable</li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="8dgi4jydw4o8tf8"><div class="body"><img id="r6Xvb" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/I6Zp_UUpnLodrOejqkq6g.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>Exposure Is Positively Correlated with Wages</h1><p>Unlike earlier tech waves that automated routine physical tasks, Gen AI reaches <b>cognitive non-routine work</b> once assumed safe from automation.</p><smart-layout variant="solidJoinedBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item id="h-FgA"><h4>Highest Exposure</h4><p> Computer &amp; Mathematical occupations (~2.0 standard units) </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="D1p6M"><h4>Lowest Exposure</h4><p> Construction &amp; Extraction (~-1.5 standard units)</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="l6ff07w85li4tek"><div class="body"><h1>The "New Moore's Law" — AI Capability Is Accelerating</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="s6dcV" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/j4ntDlciPUOsUnDfOHNXX.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><smart-layout variant="timeline" orientation="vertical" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="paXV-"><h4>GPT-2 (2019)</h4><p> 4-second task completion at 50% success rate </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="7m3-N"><h4>GPT-4 (2023)</h4><p> 6-minute tasks — passes Bar Exam at 90th percentile </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="vrVg4"><h4>2026 Forecast</h4><p> 2-hour tasks; by 2029, AI may complete a full work month (167 hrs) </p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><aside variant="info"><p>Task length AI can perform doubles every ~7 months — the "New Moore's Law"</p></aside></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="right" id="m2u4mp9xbwkgvwk"><img class="accent-image" id="pZts3xUOD9" fit-type="fit" type="image" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/TTc1HtsBvOoWH_sx93vcz.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760,&quot;isAuto&quot;:true,&quot;clipType&quot;:null,&quot;clipPath&quot;:null,&quot;clipAspectRatio&quot;:null}"><div class="body"><h1>But Experts Have Been Wrong Before</h1><ul><li>In 2021, Oxford's Michael Wooldridge listed "understanding a story," "human-level translation," and "writing interesting stories" as nowhere near solved — LLMs do all of these today</li><li>In 2016, Geoffrey Hinton said "stop training radiologists now" — radiology jobs are at <b>record highs</b></li><li>Exposure indices are a <b>moving measure</b>, not a forecast of layoffs</li></ul></div></section><section image-layout="left" id="qeuoj75wfybcak8"><img class="accent-image" id="lZv7UV2VyG" type="image" alt="bold number 03 on a dark background with glowing blue abstract technology patterns, minimal design" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/gM3rvaZEH3v1NRKvN3eOc.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>03 Layer 2: Adoption</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="yb5if93ougm1iw0"><div class="body"><h1>Exposure ≠ Adoption</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="sDhCF" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/Bpp7IAEFW6Nk98Z6APFR-.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>AI behaves like a General-Purpose Technology (GPT) — like electricity or the personal computer — whose full effects unfold gradually as firms learn to reorganize around it.</p><ul><li>A job can be highly "exposed" in theory but untouched in practice due to legal constraints, union rules, or lack of complementary investments</li><li>Electricity took decades to raise factory output; PCs took until the mid-1990s to show productivity gains</li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="qtikx1zmyvw3862"><div class="body"><h1>The Productivity J-Curve</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="5-6wc" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/HuP0wVCKUmNQ8G3JxCqUk.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>In early years after implementation, productivity often <b>dips</b> due to learning costs, setup, and process re-engineering — then overshoots as intangible gains are realized.</p><ul><li>A 2025 Harvard Business Review study found 40% of knowledge workers spent ~2 hrs/week fixing low-quality AI outputs ("workslop")</li><li>Robert Solow (1989): "You can see the computer age everywhere but in the productivity statistics."</li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="umdbu6kbco6yzga"><div class="body"><img id="SEnSU" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/U8nLTjd9fcJ0n6F_8TeL7.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>Generative AI Is Adopting Faster Than Any Prior Technology</h1><smart-layout variant="arrows" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="ZgvV5"><h4>Electricity</h4><p> 50 years: 10% → 99% household adoption </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="CfUCr"><h4>Social Media</h4><p> 15 years: 5% → 79% adoption </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="rHI0g"><h4>Generative AI</h4><p> &gt;50% adoption within 3 years of ChatGPT's 2022 launch — 54.6% adult adoption by Aug 2025</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="w6dbgdvmhlpsti3"><div class="body"><h1>Business Adoption: Fast Growth, Still Low Penetration</h1><img id="IHk39" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/nxaVPFeX3d_nan1iOwWS7.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item id="5VJCk" label="3.7%"><h4>Oct 2023</h4><p>Share of U.S. firms using AI</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="YOqBI" label="9.7%"><h4>Aug 2025</h4><p>Share of U.S. firms using AI</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><aside><p>Startups (0–5 yrs) adopt at ~7.2% vs. 2.9% for firms 21+ years old. Fewer than 10% of U.S. firms have meaningfully integrated AI into workflows.</p></aside></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="oc7yywm2dhovgpc"><div class="body"><img id="ZCaZ2" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/Sg5KV9keNtyKxelZFEYmu.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>Adoption Is Uneven by Sector</h1><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="LO-6F"><h4>💻 Software Engineering</h4><p><b>High adoption</b> — 85.4% of developers use AI tools daily, weekly, or monthly (Stack Overflow 2025) </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="Vo-P6"><h4>⚖️ Legal Services</h4><p><b>Cautious</b> — after "hallucinated" case citations (Mata vs. Avianca), judges now require AI disclosure; Cleary Gottlieb acquired Springbok AI for private deployment </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="gF4hA"><h4>🎨 Creative Arts</h4><p><b>Wary</b> — Disney, Universal, Warner Bros. sued Midjourney; SAG-AFTRA's 2023 strike reshaped AI use rules</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="right" id="cafnh5oifwxmgb4"><img class="accent-image" id="OqLlMu96MA" type="image" alt="bold number 04 on a dark background with glowing blue abstract augmentation patterns, minimal design" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/OYCMvQHF3nYE7z8OUAvaA.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>04 Layer 3: Labor Market Response — Augmentation</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="plfjqe6rbhbxevi"><div class="body"><h1>What Is Augmentation?</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="xwzLq" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/ULs8_cZgBiaZYGB-4Kjt6.png" source="image.ai-generated" alt="human hand and robotic hand working together on a glowing digital interface, collaborative technology, warm blue light" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;height&quot;:768,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>Augmentation happens when AI tools help humans do tasks they already perform — faster and better — without transferring ownership of the task to a machine.</p><ul><li>AI acts as an <b>"expertise multiplier"</b> — lifting the floor by helping less-experienced workers perform like seasoned experts</li><li>Augmentation isn't a single story: it's a spectrum depending on the task, the tool, and the worker's judgment</li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="wvt33d3farsx11b"><div class="body"><h1>Case Study 1: Knowledge Workers (Microsoft 365 Copilot)</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="5bqeE" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/gNaAnfVkibFAC5YCfDdkc.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><labels><label variant="solid">7,137 workers · 66 firms · Sept 2023–Oct 2024</label></labels><ul><li>Copilot users spent ~2 fewer hours/week on email (~17% reduction)</li><li>Time working outside regular hours fell ~9%</li></ul><aside variant="success"><p><b>Takeaway:</b> Real but narrow augmentation — speeds up text-heavy tasks but did not trigger task reallocation</p></aside></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="59v7344yqnw5fxa"><div class="body"><h1>Case Study 2: Chat Support Agents (GPT-3 Assistant)</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="vQx9L" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/VOf6ycEJ1xp4QQ7-B2_wd.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><labels><label variant="solid">5,172 agents · Fortune 500 firm</label></labels><ul><li><b>15%</b> increase in chats resolved per hour overall; <b>30%</b> increase for less-skilled workers</li><li>AI-assisted workers at 2 months performed like No-AI workers at 6 months</li></ul><aside variant="success"><p><b>Takeaway:</b> Augmentation especially lifts the bottom of the skill distribution</p></aside></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="ax5x8rh8fm6g8fg"><div class="body"><h1>Case Study 3: BCG Consultants (GPT-4)</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="BCz85" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/xU9I9vSHVuuXZ1ohfTtN4.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><labels><label variant="solid">758 BCG consultants · 18 tasks · Spring 2023</label></labels><ul><li>Below-average consultants improved <b>43%</b>; above-average improved <b>17%</b></li><li>On a "trick" task with misleading data, most consultants leaned on AI and got <b>worse</b> outcomes</li></ul><aside variant="success"><p><b>Takeaway:</b> 12.2% more tasks, ~25% faster, ~40% higher quality — but only inside the "jagged frontier"</p></aside></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="z1xew1vw2w70n6z"><div class="body"><h1>The Jagged Frontier</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="LGcje" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/DVgpVaqWxOOunPr_bxzpz.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="WwIwC"><h4>Inside the Frontier ✅</h4><p> Tasks are standardized with a "right" answer and abundant examples — AI creates biggest gains for lower-skill workers </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="Hb5lk"><h4>Outside the Frontier ⚠️</h4><p> Tasks are open-ended, novel, or outside training data — AI can mislead; experts benefit most only if they can judge output quality</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="xjbrmasnukb9ecm"><div class="body"><img id="pUa-i" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/Nc-RjWFFh4l-pfflkz__u.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>Case Study 4: Radiologists &amp; Jevons Paradox</h1><ul><li>Hinton (2016): "Stop training radiologists now" — radiology jobs are at <b>record highs</b> with avg. salary of $520,000</li><li>AI boosts productivity per scan → hospitals order more scans → more work for radiologists, not less (<b>Jevons Paradox</b>)</li><li>Radiologists also design workflows, train staff, and consult physicians — tasks AI cannot replace</li></ul></div></section><section image-layout="right" id="ly0ads2ugck20qb"><img class="accent-image" id="-ft75T2Q4w" type="image" alt="bold number 05 on a dark background with abstract displacement wave patterns in deep blue, minimal design" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/X-N2GwV53AxB3DSLj8EtD.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>05 Layer 3: Labor Market Response — Displacement</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="oa41sn6jkti6yc9"><div class="body"><h1>From Augmentation to Displacement</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="oFX-m" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/WJRa9gk62f0zJTyggh0Tv.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><ul><li>If one designer with Midjourney does the work of five concept artists, that's augmentation for one and <b>displacement for four</b></li><li><b>Historical parallel:</b> Between 1920–1940, AT&amp;T's mechanical switching eliminated telephone operator roles by 50–80% city by city</li><li>The women who built careers as operators were left behind — their skills did not transfer</li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="left" id="d5bg7enfh8pks8r"><img class="accent-image" id="haay5o7zzS" type="image" alt="corporate boardroom with executives in discussion, modern glass office, serious atmosphere, blue tones" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/y0L1qHlzZQR2cfvA2dVGP.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>Corporate Rebalancing: What Leaders Are Saying</h1><smart-layout variant="speechBubbles" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="GTYKf"><h4>Andy Jassy — Amazon</h4><p> "We expect this will reduce our total corporate workforce as we get efficiency gains from using AI extensively." </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="u6LiB"><h4>Marc Benioff — Salesforce</h4><p> Reduced customer support headcount from 9,000 to 5,000 via agentic AI agents </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="exiLQ"><h4>Duolingo (Jan 2024)</h4><p> Off-boarded 10% of contractor workforce as company pivoted to AI translation</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="47yo3l3rig3y7l1"><div class="body"><h1>The Evolution of Entry-Level Work</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="my9HJ" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/kFARZbeRxDj4DD95Xk-DO.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><img id="uQlli" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/GxEettuPYNFWC3DB-B6ns.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div></columns><ul><li>Employment for 22–25 year-old software developers fell <b>~20%</b> from peak in 2022</li><li>Same pattern for customer service reps — decline sharpest for youngest cohort</li><li>In contrast, health aides (low AI exposure) show the <b>fastest employment growth</b> among youngest workers</li></ul></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="1ku60zxyecijg8v"><div class="body"><h1>Early vs. Mid-Career: A Diverging Picture</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="-PSRH" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/WVXRdgGlbFBrKxnlO437N.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><img id="nBLsF" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/3oOuP_0dlZaP-hohAPaau.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div></columns><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="dSbCz"><h4>Early Career (22–25), More Exposed</h4><p> Headcount declined to ~0.94 by Feb 2025 </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="L5j1i"><h4>Early Career (22–25), Less Exposed</h4><p> Headcount rose sharply to ~1.15 </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="KEvTM"><h4>Mid-Career Workers</h4><p> Small differences by AI exposure — judgment and integration skills become more valuable</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="right" id="ddaruqmmd6vrs3o"><img class="accent-image" id="wNkDiRyzAi" type="image" alt="bold number 06 on a dark background with glowing blue reinstatement and growth patterns, minimal design" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/37rKGPLOUAODhUBtXhhEI.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>06 Layer 3: Labor Market Response — Reinstatement</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="8g40frnjrjlt5rw"><div class="body"><h1>What Is Reinstatement?</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="bnQto" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/VYbB5wjhYfQ3EXSheCz_G.png" source="image.ai-generated" alt="new green shoots growing from fertile soil in a modern industrial setting, metaphor for job creation, warm light" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1280,&quot;height&quot;:768,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>Reinstatement is the process by which technology creates new tasks that (partially or fully) make up for jobs lost through automation. AI-driven reinstatement takes two forms:</p></div></columns><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxes" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="yr3nV"><h4>(1) New Occupations Born from AI</h4><p> Roles that didn't meaningfully exist before the AI boom </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="bbtzq"><h4>(2) Resurgent Roles</h4><p> Existing occupations whose demand is surging because of AI infrastructure growth</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="2wzc77pjp1uhwld"><div class="body"><img id="a6E1w" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/BvAS8gOnA97D4QUuhIFEC.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><h1>New Occupations Born From AI</h1><smart-layout variant="iconsText" image-position="top" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/2Ih3tBr6D5UbaoAKIVp3.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="rocket"></icon><div><h4>AI/GenAI Engineer</h4><p> Among fastest-growing roles on LinkedIn's Jobs on the Rise 2025 — GenAI postings grew from ~0% in 2022 to ~0.28% of all postings by early 2025 </p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/TxWRISnwcpSrneKTB1k1.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="shield check"></icon><div><h4>AI Safety &amp; Governance</h4><p> "Responsible AI Lead," "AI Ethics Program Manager" — now numbering in the hundreds on U.S. job boards </p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/XTpGvXZNZMu7ZMfyTXpa.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="star badge"></icon><div><h4>Chief AI Officer (CAIO)</h4><p> Companies with this role have roughly <b>tripled</b> in 5 years</p></div></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="9r9m5csus3gu1vb"><div class="body"><h1>Resurgent Roles: The Physical Infrastructure of AI</h1><img id="OtdOw" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/tzSRaoNEhFHStKEo-vLlj.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="F-wIL" label="$40B"><h4>Annual Rate</h4><p>U.S. data center construction spending (June 2025 record)</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="PKTbU" label="5,000+"><h4>Construction Workers</h4><p>Meta's Louisiana AI hub at peak; Microsoft's Wisconsin center: ~2,300 union jobs</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="PG_r8" label="30%"><h4>Pay Increases</h4><p>Data center workers seeing 25–30% raises, some breaking into six-figure salaries</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="4bujkhqzbnichr0"><div class="body"><h1>The Skilled Trades Gap</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="qCN6e" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/B_s4ZKKyc-dWAtnMaQt3X.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><ul><li>Today: <b>~400,000 unfilled skilled-trade roles</b> in the U.S. as data centers demand electricians, HVAC techs, and skilled labor</li><li>By 2033: projected <b>1.9 million unfilled manufacturing jobs</b> due to skill gaps</li><li>Resurgent roles (electricians, pipefitters, heavy equipment operators) projected to grow from 15K in 2023 to <b>140K+ by 2030</b></li></ul></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="right" id="ch1220dy7q0flpg"><img class="accent-image" id="b3AoWjzZlK" type="image" alt="bold number 07 on a dark background with glowing blue feedback loop cycle patterns, minimal design" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/CT8yNcmcWJkd9itNbr56P.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>07 The AI-Work Feedback Cycle</h1></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="talvsqwc9je81sq"><div class="body"><h1>Feedback Loops: A Living System</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img id="Sl2q-" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/g3PcO9ERUoNj6qpB7fws2.png" source="image.custom" align="center" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>New capabilities determine exposure → exposure invites or deters adoption → adoption reshapes hiring, skills, and wages → labor market outcomes push back on what gets built next.</p></div></columns><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="T3P1K"><h4>Software Loop</h4><p> LLM coding improvements → rapid developer adoption → usage data → better coding models </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="OL7oO"><h4>Law Loop</h4><p> Hallucination errors → cautious adoption → ethics guidance → pressure on AI labs to improve accuracy</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="left" id="pjgpe1ay1fljs3i"><img class="accent-image" id="o6ptGhgpSn" type="image" alt="person standing at a crossroads in a modern city with glowing digital signage, decisive moment, blue and orange tones" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/generated-images/D7iki9_eCpvA006uAlag1.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:864,&quot;height&quot;:1152,&quot;has_transparency&quot;:false}"><div class="body"><h1>What This Means for You</h1><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="XeDOA"><h4>Early-Career in an Exposed Role</h4><p> Adapt now — learn to use AI tools, focus on judgment and verification skills </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="GN0Bf"><h4>Mid-Career</h4><p> Your experience is a moat — AI lifts juniors toward you, making your oversight and decision-making more valuable </p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="btZly"><h4>Physical Trades</h4><p> AI infrastructure buildout is creating unprecedented demand — and pay increases of 25–30% </p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><aside variant="info"><p>The question is not whether AI will change work — it's whether <b>you'll be the one using it</b>.</p></aside></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="nr4iiu2qjwhjm38"><div class="body"><h1>Sources</h1><ul><li><a href="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/QdGUaRFSVYeVktpN6Z2M_.png">Deep Dive: AI and the Labor Market (PDF)</a></li></ul></div></section>